<article class="env-definition" id="04JP">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04JP">Definition <span data-tag="04JP">59.57.1</span>.</a> Let $G$ be a topological group. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
A <em>$G$-module</em>, sometimes called a <em>discrete $G$-module</em>, is an abelian group $M$ endowed with a left action $a : G \times M \to M$ by group homomorphisms such that $a$ is continuous when $M$ is given the discrete topology. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A <em>morphism of $G$-modules</em> $f : M \to N$ is a $G$-equivariant homomorphism from $M$ to $N$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The category of $G$-modules is denoted $\text{Mod}_ G$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Let $R$ be a ring. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
An <em>$R\text{-}G$-module</em> is an $R$-module $M$ endowed with a left action $a : G \times M \to M$ by $R$-linear maps such that $a$ is continuous when $M$ is given the discrete topology. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A <em>morphism of $R\text{-}G$-modules</em> $f : M \to N$ is a $G$-equivariant $R$-module map from $M$ to $N$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The category of $R\text{-}G$-modules is denoted $\text{Mod}_{R, G}$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>